2026-06-23 00:11:08,999 | INFO | HTTP Request: POST http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/chat "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
2026-06-23 00:11:08,999 | AGENT | AIInnovation Agent answered: To meet the client's requirements for an artificial-intelligence assistance solution that improves productivity and quality of work, I propose leveraging our company's Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) capabilities. This approach will enable the generation of answers grounded in validated material such as accounting standards, tax doctrine, and internal procedures, thereby limiting unsupported generation and enhancing reliability.

Our platform, Atelier, can be utilized to develop a hybrid retrieval system that combines lexical and semantic search, allowing for more accurate and context-specific information retrieval. This can be further enhanced with re-ranking, query reformulation, and multi-document synthesis capabilities to provide comprehensive and relevant responses to professionals' queries.

The use of template-guided drafting will facilitate the creation of client letters, mission notes, and file syntheses, supporting daily activities and improving productivity. Moreover, our system's ability to perform structured extraction with source linking will enable the accurate retrieval of figures from documents and link them back to their source passages, ensuring the reliability of numeric outputs.

To address explainability, our features such as source citations, retrieved passages, retrieval trace, and logged interactions for posterior audit will provide transparency into how the AI arrives at its suggestions. This human oversight built into the workflow ensures that professionals validate and sign off on deliverables, maintaining control over the decision-making process.

Given the client's constraints, particularly those related to data sovereignty, confidentiality, and professional secrecy, our sovereign European open-weight model (self-hosted) would be an appropriate choice. This option provides full auditability and is well-suited for reliability-driven and sovereignty-driven requirements, albeit with somewhat lower raw performance on complex tasks compared to top-tier proprietary models.

However, there are potential innovation risks and limitations. One key risk is the trade-off between performance and explainability; while our RAG approach enhances transparency, it might not achieve the same level of performance as more opaque models. Additionally, the reliance on high-quality, curated corpora for training and retrieval means that the system's effectiveness could be compromised if the underlying data is incomplete, outdated, or inaccurate.

Another limitation could arise from the fine-tuning process, where overfitting to specific client data might reduce the model's generalizability across different contexts or tasks. It's also crucial to monitor the environmental footprint of our solution, ensuring it aligns with the client's environmental sustainability goals and our own commitment to responsible AI development.

Lastly, as we move forward with deploying this solution, it will be essential to conduct thorough evaluations and gather feedback from professionals using the system. This iterative approach will help identify any unforeseen challenges or areas for improvement, allowing us to refine the AI components and better meet the client's evolving needs over time.
